# {{ polity.title or "(polity missing)" }}

{% if is_polity_root %}
{{ polity.title or "The polity" }} is a confederacy for Black Liberation and Indigenous Sovereignty (founded 2018-04-01). This repository is the polity's *meta-repo* — it holds the manifest and bootstrap for the family of repositories that together form the polity's operational and intellectual life. Each member retains its own git history; this repo coordinates them.

## Layout

```
{{ polity_layout.short or "polity" }}/
├── polity.yaml                       manifest of every member repo
├── Makefile                          install and verify the local operator tools
├── theories/                         theory corpus
├── records/                          operational records
└── tools/                            runtime, platform, publishing, and external source repositories
```

Root and nested repositories are tracked as Git submodules and described in `polity.yaml`. Nested tool repositories such as `tools/platform/pyfars/` and `tools/math/pypred/` live inside the `tools` submodule. The platform-slug taxonomy (`tools/platform/<realization>/`) mirrors the engineering-theory taxonomy in `{{ top_theories_root_path }}/engineering/.../predicated-computing/`.

## Bootstrap

```bash
git clone --recurse-submodules {{ polity_layout.meta_repo_url or "git@github.com:emsenn/561.git" }}
cd {{ polity_layout.short or "561" }}
git submodule update --init --recursive # idempotent repair if clone omitted recursion
make install                            # installs the pyfars command through Nix
nix run .#pyfars                        # runs host-aware PyFARS bootstrap for this checkout
```

Without submodules, you can read `polity.yaml` and `git clone` each repo manually into its declared path, but the normal path is recursive submodule clone/update.

`make install` is the normal operator bootstrap. It installs `pyfars` into the user's Nix profile from the root flake, using the local `tools/platform/pyfars/` checkout as the input when that sibling is present. After this step, `pyfars` should be available as a normal shell command.

`nix run .#pyfars` with no arguments runs the host-aware bootstrap app. With arguments, it dispatches to the normal PyFARS CLI, so `nix run .#pyfars -- run .` is equivalent to running `pyfars run .` from an installed profile.

