The live command surface is installed with `make install`, which uses the flake's `pyfars` package. `nix run .#pyfars` runs the generated host-aware bootstrap when invoked with no arguments. Adding or changing a host means editing the corresponding record under `{{ command_root_path }}/group/computer-systems/` and re-running `pyfars install-harness nix-flake`; the generated flake should reflect the records, not invent host policy on its own.

### Installing Nix

The flake assumes a working multi-user Nix install. We use **[Determinate Nix](https://determinate.systems/)** as the canonical installer:

```bash
curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -sSf -L https://install.determinate.systems/nix | sh -s -- install
```

Reasons: flakes enabled by default, robust uninstall, and a more agent-friendly maintenance surface than the Ubuntu/Debian-packaged Nix (which we found to be broken on first install at `vps-mini-cold-red-gate` — see the friction-log). After install, open a new shell so `PATH` picks up `/nix/var/nix/profiles/default/bin` and `~/.nix-profile/bin`, then:

```bash
make install
pyfars --help
```

If your host is registered as a polity computer-system, `nix develop .#<your-computer-system-slug>` gives a development shell for repair and local development. If it is not registered, `nix develop` gives a generic devshell with the same toolchain.

### Why Nix carries the swarm, and not (yet) something else

The captain's preference is Guix; Nix is today's pragma. The flake-as-deployment-spec story is mature in Nix in a way Guix's manifest story isn't yet, and getting the polity's swarm-projection working *now* matters more than getting the long-term-correct packaging story right. A future Guix harness will likely wrap the Nix layer rather than replace it.

