## Tooling

`pyfars` is the polity's substrate runtime. The CLI is lifecycle-only — Docker / Apache / systemd shaped:

- `pyfars run <dir>` — boot the engine bound to the polity at `<dir>`. Spawns services declared by the substrate (web app, MCP server, control-plane API on a Unix Domain Socket at `<dir>/.pyfars/run.sock`).
- `pyfars install` — install harness files into a polity-image.
- `pyfars clean` — undo install.
- `pyfars help [<verb>]` — usage.

Operations on a *running* polity (substrate queries, witness emission, dispatch, etc.) go through the engine's API rather than the CLI. The same FastAPI app is served simultaneously over UDS (for local clients) and TCP (for the public web). Endpoints include `/api/show/<slug>`, `/api/context/<slug>`, `/api/search`, `/api/list`, `/api/stats`, `/api/control/engine`. See `governance/specifications/wiring-protocols.md` (in pyfars) for the full protocol map.

Run `make install` from the repository root to install `pyfars` as a normal command in the user's Nix profile. See the [pyfars repository's README](https://github.com/emsenn/pyfars#readme) for the full toolkit guide.

